# Void Settings Architecture

## Central Settings Service

`voidSettingsService` (common/) is the **single source of truth** for all Void configuration.

It's an implicit dependency for most core Void services - treat it as foundational infrastructure.

## Settings State Structure

```typescript
VoidSettingsState {
  settingsOfProvider          // Provider configs (API keys, endpoints, available models)
  modelSelectionOfFeature     // Selected model per feature (Chat, CtrlK, Apply, Autocomplete, SCM)
  optionsOfModelSelection     // Per-model options (temperature, maxTokens, etc.)
  globalSettings              // Global Void settings (chatMode, autoApprove, etc.)
  overridesOfModel            // Model capability overrides
  mcpUserStateOfName          // MCP server state (enabled/disabled per server)
}
```

## Key Terminology

- **FeatureName** - `Autocomplete | Chat | CtrlK | Apply | SCM`
- **ModelSelection** - A `{providerName, modelName}` pair
- **ProviderName** - Provider identifier string (`'ollama'`, `'openAI'`, `'anthropic'`, etc.)
- **ModelName** - Model identifier string (`'gpt-4o'`, `'claude-sonnet-4'`, etc.)
- **RefreshProvider** - Provider that we ping repeatedly to update available models list
- **ChatMode** - `normal | gather | agent`

## State Updates

Settings use **immutable state updates** - always create new state objects rather than mutating existing ones.

## Model Capabilities

**IMPORTANT**: `modelCapabilities.ts` must be updated when new models are released.

This file defines:

- Available models per provider
- Model capabilities (vision, streaming, context windows)
- Default settings per model

## ChatMode

Configurable per thread or globally via Case Organizer:

- **normal** - Standard chat interaction
- **gather** - Case research mode (legal focus)
- **agent** - Autonomous agent mode with tool use
